// The Nine Chambers.
//
// From the wing's research file aiq-bekar.verified.json, whose fact-check read
// the English of 1651 in the EEBO-TCP keyed text A26565 (CC0). Every English
// passage below is J.F.'s and was checked again there, word for word; where
// the transcription is uncertain (“interfection”, “itbe … poynt”) or keys the
// Hebrew only as a gap, the words are left out and the omission is marked,
// not guessed. The Latin chapter heading of 1533, and the place of the
// chambers in III.30 of 1533 after the three alphabets, are from the later
// check of the print in the-books.verified.json (Library of Congress copy).
//
// The figures are traced from the cut of 1533: the row of nine figures under
// the grid on p. CCLXXV, McGill copy (Internet Archive leaf n290), the
// sharpest image of the setting it shares, block for block, with the Library
// of Congress copy; the points from Michael's figures lower on the same page
// (traced/aiq-bekar/glyphs.json, independently checked). Only five letters
// are printed with their points (Mem, Yod, Kaf, Aleph, Lamed); the points of
// the other twenty-two are the site's, placed by the rule and by Michael's
// figures, and those letters are formConfidence medium. Each glyph is the
// traced path data unchanged, so the page draws it exactly as the tracer's
// specimen sheet does. The research's diagram paths, made from the rule under
// an assumed layout, are not used. The McGill digitisation carries no rights
// statement, so the plate shows the same page of the Library of Congress
// copy, whose repository states no known restriction.
//
// The Hebrew hall counts the five finals at the value of their ordinary
// letters; in this system they are the hundreds 500–900 (Agrippa II.19). So
// their `numeric` is left null (the validator checks stored numbers against
// the hall) and each final's note gives its value here.
